sent: `True`\n- placed_after_references: `True`\n- pre_bibliography_clearpage_present: `True`\n- clearpage_present: `True`\n- visible_character_count: `5822`\n- substantive_element_count: `7`\n- rendered_checked: `False`\n- rendered_heading_present: `False`\n- rendered_visible_character_count: `0`\n\n## Evidence validation\n- bundle_path: `work/results/a01/claude/20261111_071545/paper/scientific-evidence`\n- bundle_present: `True`\n- verification_report: `work/results/a01/claude/20261111_071545/paper/scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- total_references: `46`\n- resolved_references: `21`\n- anchored_references: `25`\n- dangling_references: `0`\n- manifests_checked: `5`\n\n## Agentic evidence audit\n- checked: `True`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `1`\n- summary: Numbers, qualification facts and development-comparison figures match the evidence trail. One claim credits the held-out advantage over naive baselines to the per-rule solvers, which the evidence does not isolate.\n\n### Audit findings to fix\n- `h1-solver-attribution-1` (Results (Held-out evaluation, Status of the hypotheses Table tab:status) and Design rationale H1): The held-out comparison is between the whole frozen planner and the evaluator's naive baselines. The planner is the base solvers plus verification, pruning, boundary polish and the uniform-gain fallback. No held-out ablation separates the per-rule solvers from those other components. The development comparison shows polish alone changes main efficiency (1.0031 to 1.0051), so the 1.0032 vs 0.8166 gap cannot be assigned to per-rule solvers specifically. The claim is an isolated causal attribution that no controlled comparison supports.\n  - claim: H1 'Verified per-rule solvers have higher main efficiency than the better of uniform-gain and greedy marginal-cost baselines' is marked 'Supported', and the text says 'the comparison shows that the solvers matter for cost'\n  - evidence: claims_grounding.json claims[1-2] ground only the planner-vs-baseline metrics (final.metric.main_efficiency 1.0031855, final.metric.baseline_main_efficiency 0.8165814). paper_data.json final_evaluation covers one frozen candidate (program tree db5d072f). source_evidence/planner_boundary_polish_source.py is the base plus the polish stage. paper.tex states 'The held-out study evaluates one frozen planner: base solvers plus boundary polishing'. No held-out per-component metric exists.\n  - requested_fix: Restate H1 and the Table tab:status row as 'the frozen verified planner (base solvers, verification, pruning and polish) exceeds the better naive baseline on the two held-out boards'. Remove or qualify 'the comparison shows that the solvers matter for cost'. Add that no held-out ablation separates the per-rule solvers from verification, pruning, polish and the uniform fallback.\n\n## Soft warnings\n- figures[0].font_sizes.title below minimum: 8.5 < 10.0\n- figures[0].font_sizes.axis below minimum: 8.0 < 9.0\n- figures[1].font_sizes.title below minimum: 8.5 < 10.0\n- figures[1].font_sizes.axis below minimum: 8.0 < 9.0\n- figures[1] PNG width too small: 1650 < 2400\n- figures[2].font_sizes.title below minimum: 8.5 < 10.0\n- figures[2].font_sizes.axis below minimum: 8.0 < 9.0\n- figures[2] PNG width too small: 1650 < 2400\n# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. The draft is mostly well-supported. Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are not flagged. Three targeted retrieval needs remain.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Priced, swap and weighted bribery under Copeland, Borda and plurality.\n- Open LLM Leaderboard (v2) documentation.\n- Goodhart's law and metric gaming in ML evaluation.\n\n## Rewrite targets\n\n### 1. `related-work-social-choice-bribery` (related work, high, comparator)\n- **Anchor:** \"Our problem is a continuous variant: the 'voters' are tasks, the intervention changes one candidate's scores...\"\n- **Why:** The paper positions itself against manipulation, bribery and margin-of-victory problems, but the cited works are generic or about election attacks. It cites nothing on cost-priced or swap bribery, or on bribery and control under Copeland, Borda and plurality. Those are the closest comparators for the rules the planner handles heuristically.\n- **Search topics:** priced and swap bribery; Copeland, Borda and plurality bribery and control; bribery under score-based voting with per-voter costs.\n\n### 2. `heldout-data-open-llm-leaderboard` (related work, high, benchmark_context)\n- **Anchor:** \"The Open LLM Leaderboard aggregates six benchmarks\"\n- **Why:** The whole held-out evaluation uses Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots. The draft cites only the component benchmarks, not the leaderboard itself.\n- **Search topics:** Open LLM Leaderboard v2 description; evaluation-harness documentation.\n\n### 3. `intro-incentive-gaming-claim` (introduction, medium, background)\n- **Anchor:** \"Rankings influence adoption, publication and funding, so developers have an incentive to act on them\"\n- **Why:** This is the premise of the motivating question and it has no support. One or two Goodhart or leaderboard-incentive references would be enough.\n- **Search topics:** Goodhart's law and metric gaming in ML benchmarks; benchmark culture and leaderboard incentives.\n\n## Deliberately not flagged\n- Software citations (NumPy, SciPy, MILP solver). These are low value for the narrative.\n- Planner heuristics such as bisection and greedy search. They are standard and described in the paper itself.\n- The contamination and leaderboard-gaming paragraph, which is already densely cited.\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"06f24fa2-63d3-4bc9-bb45-b72aa78f77ec","uuid":"7223efba-64f1-417b-ba71-b5de617246b9","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:31:51.490Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 4928\ndrwx------ 8 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 09:31 .\ndrwx------ 9 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 09:28 ..\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     751 Nov 11 08:40 .agentic_paper_workspace.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:40 .claude\n-rw------- 1 agent agent       0 Nov 11 09:30 .concept_figure_complete\nprw------- 1 agent agent       0 Nov 11 09:31 .paper_repair_round_2_stream_20261111_084048.fifo\n-rw--