{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016Twp915PtBqcUVkePVRB1u","type":"tool_result","content":"# Agentic evidence audit feedback\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-11T09:31:43.923784+00:00`\n- ok: `False`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `1`\n- audit_json_path: `work/results/a01/claude/20261111_071545/paper/paper_evidence_audit.json`\n- audit_markdown_path: `work/results/a01/claude/20261111_071545/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md`\n- raw_output_path: `work/results/a01/claude/20261111_071545/paper/paper_evidence_audit_raw.txt`\n\n## Summary\nNumbers, qualification facts and development-comparison figures match the evidence trail. One claim credits the held-out advantage over naive baselines to the per-rule solvers, which the evidence does not isolate.\n\n## Findings\n- `h1-solver-attribution-1` (Results (Held-out evaluation, Status of the hypotheses Table tab:status) and Design rationale H1): The held-out comparison is between the whole frozen planner and the evaluator's naive baselines. The planner is the base solvers plus verification, pruning, boundary polish and the uniform-gain fallback. No held-out ablation separates the per-rule solvers from those other components. The development comparison shows polish alone changes main efficiency (1.0031 to 1.0051), so the 1.0032 vs 0.8166 gap cannot be assigned to per-rule solvers specifically. The claim is an isolated causal attribution that no controlled comparison supports.\n  - claim: H1 'Verified per-rule solvers have higher main efficiency than the better of uniform-gain and greedy marginal-cost baselines' is marked 'Supported', and the text says 'the comparison shows that the solvers matter for cost'\n  - evidence: claims_grounding.json claims[1-2] ground only the planner-vs-baseline metrics (final.metric.main_efficiency 1.0031855, final.metric.baseline_main_efficiency 0.8165814). paper_data.json final_evaluation covers one frozen candidate (program tree db5d072f). source_evidence/planner_boundary_polish_source.py is the base plus the polish stage. paper.tex states 'The held-out study evaluates one frozen planner: base solvers plus boundary polishing'. No held-out per-component metric exists.\n  - requested_fix: Restate H1 and the Table tab:status row as 'the frozen verified planner (base solvers, verification, pruning and polish) exceeds the better naive baseline on the two held-out boards'. Remove or qualify 'the comparison shows that the solvers matter for cost'. Add that no held-out ablation separates the per-rule solvers from verification, pruning, polish and the uniform fallback.\n{\n \"latex_logs\": []\n}\nUnderfull \\vbox (badness 2269) has occurred while \\output is active []\n\n\n\n[7 <./figures/search_variant_comparison.pdf>]\n\nPackage natbib Warning: Citation `Zhou2023DontMY' on page 8 undefined on input \nline 206.\n\n\nPackage natbib Warning: Citation `Sainz2023NLPEI' on page 8 undefined on input \nline 206.\n\n\n\n[8]\n\nLaTeX Warning: Reference `app:repro' on page 9 undefined on input line 221.\n\n\n\n[9] (./paper.bbl\nUnderfull \\hbox (badness 1629) in paragraph at lines 133--138\n[]\\OT1/ptm/m/n/10 Open LLM Leader-board main-tain-ers.  Open llm leader-board: \ncon-tents dataset (snap-shot\n []\n\n! Misplaced alignment tab character &.\nl.143 ...ck Gpqa: A graduate-level google-proof q&\n                                                  a benchmark. \nHere is how much of TeX's memory you used:\n 13014 strings out of 475178\n 205303 string characters out of 5766538\n 615713 words of memory out of 5000000\n 35753 multiletter control sequences out of 15000+600000\n 580854 words of font info for 80 fonts, out of 8000000 for 9000\n 14 hyphenation exceptions out of 8191\n 72i,12n,79p,1380b,527s stack positions out of 10000i,1000n,20000p,200000b,200000s\n\n!  ==> Fatal error occurred, no output PDF file produced!\n38:Multi-task leaderboards turn a matrix of per-task scores into a single ranking through an aggregation rule, and a developer who can raise a target model's score on selected tasks, for example by training on related data, may be able to push it to first place. How much such targeted investment is needed, and how that quantity depends on the aggregation rule, is the question that motivates this work. We formalize targeted investment as non-negative, independent per-task score gains, priced either by total points (L1) or by the number of tasks touched (L0), with success meaning the target is strictly first under eight rules (mean, median, trimmed mean, z-score mean, Borda, plurality, Copeland, maximin). We then study a verification-first planner that returns the cheapest plan it can certify, combining exact solvers for four rules with verified heuristics for the others. On two held-out Open LLM Leaderboard boards, scored by a trusted evaluator against its own reference solver, the planner reaches a mean efficiency of 1.0032 relative to the reference, against 0.8166 for the better of two naive baselines, with every response well-formed; the evaluation is qualified. Because per-rule costs were not retained, we do \\emph{not} test whether required investment varies across rules, and the results say nothing about real training effects.\n44:This paper takes a first, deliberately narrow step: it makes the question computable and checks that the computation can be done reliably. We (i) define a declared investment model and two cost measures, (ii) specify eight aggregation rules and the strict-first success criterion, and (iii) study a planner whose every output is re-verified against an exact replica of the rule before it is returned. The planner is assessed on held-out public leaderboard snapshots by an evaluator that owns the rule engine, the plan verifier, a reference solver, naive baselines and control conditions; candidate-reported diagnostics are never trusted.\n46:\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} The held-out evaluation completed and is qualified: the planner's main efficiency exceeds that of the naive baselines and all integrity checks passed. The measured quantity is \\emph{cost-efficiency of the planner relative to an evaluator-owned reference solver} under the declared investment model. It is not a measurement of how much investment real developers need, and the retained metrics contain no per-rule or per-target cost tables. The hypothesis that minimal required investment varies with the aggregation rule therefore remains a motivating hypothesis here, neither supported nor refuted.\n51:\\item A verification-first planner (exact solvers for mean, median, maximin and plurality; verified heuristics for trimmed mean, z-score mean, Borda and Copeland; plan verification, pruning, boundary polishing and a safe fallback) whose design is documented at the level of the evaluated source (Section~\\ref{sec:planner}).\n52:\\item A held-out evaluation on two Open LLM Leaderboard boards against a reference solver, naive baselines and two controls, with a separate development comparison of three planner configurations on HELM MMLU (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n85:Table~\\ref{tab:rules} lists the eight rules, with the planner's solution method from its source and the reference solver's method from the evaluator source. Mean, median, trimmed mean (dropping the lowest and highest 20\\% of tasks), maximin (worst-task score) and z-score mean (per-task standardization recomputed on the modified matrix) act on score levels; Borda (mean per-task rank), plurality (number of tasks on which a model is strictly first) and Copeland (pairwise task-majority wins) act on per-task orderings and are piecewise constant in $g$. Raising the target's own scores can only help it under all rules except z-score mean, because that rule re-standardizes each column using the modified matrix and so can move rivals' values in either direction; the planner's source treats it as non-monotone.\n94:Rule & Acts on & Planner & Reference solver\\\\\n112:\\emph{Verification before return.} Discrete rules flip at exact floating-point ties, and heuristics that shave a plan to the boundary can land on the wrong side by rounding. Returning only plans that an exact replica of the rule confirms removes this failure mode at the cost of extra evaluations, and it makes the planner's output trustworthy independently of the solver that proposed it. \\emph{Rule-specific structure.} A uniform gain ignores which tasks are cheap to lift; solvers that exploit the structure of each rule (Table~\\ref{tab:rules}) should find cheaper plans than a rule-agnostic fill. \\emph{Boundary polishing.} A plan returned by a constructive heuristic is rarely tight, so shrinking each gain to the verification boundary, dropping redundant tasks and moving points between tasks should lower cost further, and for L0 two-for-one swaps can reduce the number of treated tasks. We state three hypotheses:\n114:\\item[H1 (solver advantage).] Verified per-rule solvers have higher main efficiency than the better of uniform-gain and greedy marginal-cost baselines on held-out boards.\n115:\\item[H2 (polish).] Boundary polishing lowers verified plan cost relative to the base solvers on a development leaderboard.\n116:\\item[H3 (MILP selection).] A MILP level-selection layer lowers cost for the discrete rules (Borda, Copeland, plurality) and the trimmed mean relative to the base solvers.\n118:H1 is the only hypothesis tested on held-out data. H2 and H3 are tested only in the development comparison with two seeds. The question of rule-dependence in required investment is not among the tested hypotheses (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n124:\\item \\textbf{Per-rule candidate plans.} Exact solvers are used for mean, median, maximin and plurality; verified heuristics (floor-family plans, convex-hull and ratio greedy, restarted greedy for L0) for trimmed mean, z-score mean, Borda and Copeland (Table~\\ref{tab:rules}). A uniform-gain plan (L1) or headroom-ordered fill (L0) is always added as a candidate.\n132:The evaluator owns the rule engine, the plan verifier, the reference solver, the naive baselines and the controls. The \\emph{episode efficiency} of a verified successful plan is $\\min\\{2,\\,\\max(\\mathrm{ref},\\epsilon)/\\max(\\mathrm{cost},\\epsilon)\\}$, where $\\mathrm{ref}$ is the reference solver's cost and costs below 0.01 points count as 0.01 (L0 costs are counts). A correct infeasibility claim, or a correct empty plan for an already-first target, scores 1; any other outcome, including malformed or timed-out responses, scores 0 and is never retried or dropped. The reference solver is exact for mean, maximin and odd-$T$ median and search-based otherwise, and its candidate set includes the polished naive baselines for L1; hence an average efficiency above 1 means the planner's verified plans were on average slightly cheaper than the reference plans, not that the reference is optimal.\n138:\\paragraph{Compared configurations.} The held-out study evaluates one frozen planner: base solvers plus boundary polishing. For a development-only component comparison, three configurations were scored on HELM MMLU: the base solvers (Base); the base solvers with an additional MILP level-selection layer for Borda, Copeland, plurality and trimmed mean and an exchange polish for z-score L0 (Base+MILP); and the base solvers with boundary polishing (Base+Polish). The Base+Polish source does not contain the MILP layer, so these are alternative extensions of the base rather than a cumulative chain. Only Base+Polish was carried into the held-out study. No development-plane baseline metric was retained.\n150:The held-out evaluation completed and its outcome is qualified. The planner's main efficiency is 1.0032 relative to the reference solver, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.main_efficiency]]\n158:Three observations bound the interpretation. First, an efficiency near 1 means that the planner's verified plans cost, on average, about the same as the reference plans; the retained value does not reveal how many cases sit exactly at 1, how many are above, or whether any case is below. Second, the planner's candidate set includes the uniform-gain and headroom-fill plans, so a large share of the gap to the baselines is expected from per-rule solvers; the comparison shows that the solvers matter for cost, not that this planner is better than other sophisticated planners, which were not evaluated. Third, the two evaluator seeds do not resample the leaderboard: they change permutation and shuffle draws and restart randomness, so the 3.5$\\times10^{-5}$ spread between seeds reflects solver and control variability, not data sampling, and no confidence interval or significance test can be formed from two values.\n191:H1: solvers beat naive baselines & Supported & Held-out main efficiency 1.0032 vs.\\ 0.8166; two boards, two seeds; planner pool includes the naive plan\\\\\n208:\\paragraph{Reference and baselines.} The reference solver is exact only for some rules, so efficiency is relative, not optimal; a value above 1 is possible and was observed on average. Efficiency is capped at 2 per episode, which bounds the influence of individual cases, and costs under 0.01 points are floored, which hides tie-breaking differences. The planner was not compared with generic integer-programming or black-box optimization baselines other than the MILP variant on the development data.\n219:We formalized targeted investment on multi-task leaderboards and evaluated a verification-first planner that returns only plans re-checked against an exact replica of the aggregation rule. On two held-out Open LLM Leaderboard boards, the planner's mean efficiency relative to an evaluator-owned reference solver was 1.0032, against 0.8166 for the better naive baseline, with a plan validity rate of 1.0 and a qualified outcome. A development comparison on HELM MMLU suggests that boundary polishing improves cost slightly (combined 1.00339 versus 1.00183 for the base solvers) at higher compute, whereas an additional MILP level-selection layer (with a z-score exchange polish) changed nothing measurable. These conclusions are conditional on the declared investment model, rest on two evaluator seeds, and concern planner cost-efficiency. The central question that motivated the study, how much required investment varies with the aggregation rule, needs per-rule, per-target cost tables with paired uncertainty and the shuffled-null comparison; these remain future work, as do models with spillover and heterogeneous task costs.\n276:Base solvers & 1.0031 & 0.9967 & 1.00183 & 1.0 & 9.3, 8.8\\\\ % @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/config_01_metrics.json]]\n284:The evaluated source has three layers. (i) \\emph{Exact replica.} \\texttt{verify\\_ranks\\_first} re-applies the gains to the target row and recomputes the rule on the full matrix; a margin of $2\\times10^{-8}$ is demanded locally, above the evaluator's $10^{-9}$. (ii) \\emph{Candidates.} Per-rule solvers, plus the starter uniform plan, are verified, pruned and sorted by cost. (iii) \\emph{Polish.} Up to six passes of shrink, drop and pairwise-transfer moves are applied within 2\\,s, and the result is accepted only if verified and not costlier. The MILP variant, evaluated on the development leaderboard only, adds a level-selection integer program (with a 4\\,s solver budget per episode) whose plan is accepted only after verification, so it can only tie or improve on the heuristic plan; the retained scores are identical to Base. Implementation details come from the included source files \\texttt{source\\_evidence/planner\\_boundary\\_polish\\_source.py}, \\texttt{planner\\_milp\\_variant\\_source.py} and \\texttt{planner\\_base\\_source.py}.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"06f24fa2-63d3-4bc9-bb45-b72aa78f77ec","uuid":"4c6a2577-5057-40c1-93da-0bbeaf338853","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:31:54.448Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Agentic evidence aud