{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SKKKdoQjtQJDeH9KrwMtKd","type":"tool_result","content":"201: title = {GPQA: A Graduate-Level Google-Proof Q\\&A Benchmark},\n\\emph{Verification before return.} Discrete rules flip at exact floating-point ties, and heuristics that shave a plan to the boundary can land on the wrong side by rounding. Returning only plans that an exact replica of the rule confirms removes this failure mode at the cost of extra evaluations, and it makes the planner's output trustworthy independently of the solver that proposed it. \\emph{Rule-specific structure.} A uniform gain ignores which tasks are cheap to lift; solvers that exploit the structure of each rule (Table~\\ref{tab:rules}) should find cheaper plans than a rule-agnostic fill. \\emph{Boundary polishing.} A plan returned by a constructive heuristic is rarely tight, so shrinking each gain to the verification boundary, dropping redundant tasks and moving points between tasks should lower cost further, and for L0 two-for-one swaps can reduce the number of treated tasks. We state three hypotheses:\n\\begin{description}\n\\item[H1 (solver advantage).] Verified per-rule solvers have higher main efficiency than the better of uniform-gain and greedy marginal-cost baselines on held-out boards.\n\\item[H2 (polish).] Boundary polishing lowers verified plan cost relative to the base solvers on a development leaderboard.\n\\item[H3 (MILP selection).] A MILP level-selection layer lowers cost for the discrete rules (Borda, Copeland, plurality) and the trimmed mean relative to the base solvers.\n\\end{description}\nH1 is the only hypothesis tested on held-out data. H2 and H3 are tested only in the development comparison with two seeds. The question of rule-dependence in required investment is not among the tested hypotheses (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n\\caption{Held-out evaluation on two Open LLM Leaderboard boards (two evaluator seeds). (a) Main efficiency of the verified planner and of the better of two naive baselines per case, against the reference level of 1. (b) Main, control and combined efficiency of the planner on a zoomed axis; dots are the two per-seed combined values.}\n\\label{fig:final}\n\\end{figure}\n\nThe held-out evaluation completed and its outcome is qualified. The planner's main efficiency is 1.0032 relative to the reference solver, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.main_efficiency]]\nversus 0.8166 for the better naive baseline per case, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_main_efficiency]]\na difference of 0.187 (Figure~\\ref{fig:final}a). The plan validity rate is 1.0, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.plan_validity_rate]]\nso no episode was malformed, timed out or out of bounds. Control efficiency is 1.0002, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_efficiency]]\nand the combined score is 1.0026. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.combined_score]]\nPer-seed combined scores were 1.00260 and 1.00257 for seeds 9001 and 9002, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\nand each seed individually satisfies the gate that main efficiency exceeds the baseline's.\n\nThree observations bound the interpretation. First, an efficiency near 1 means that the planner's verified plans cost, on average, about the same as the reference plans; the retained value does not reveal how many cases sit exactly at 1, how many are above, or whether any case is below. Second, the planner's candidate set includes the uniform-gain and headroom-fill plans, so a large share of the gap to the baselines is expected from per-rule solvers; the comparison shows that the solvers matter for cost, not that this planner is better than other sophisticated planners, which were not evaluated. Third, the two evaluator seeds do not resample the leaderboard: they change permutation and shuffle draws and restart randomness, so the 3.5$\\times10^{-5}$ spread between seeds reflects solver and control variability, not data sampling, and no confidence interval or significance test can be formed from two values.\n\n\\paragraph{Reading an efficiency near 1.} The held-out main efficiency averages 64 main cases per seed (8 rules, 2 target ranks, 2 cost measures, 2 boards), each with weight $1/64\\approx0.0156$. An excess of 0.0032 over 1 is therefore about one fifth of the credit a single case would add at the maximal per-episode efficiency of 2, and about 0.2 of a case-unit in total. The retained aggregate cannot distinguish many tiny savings relative to the reference from a few larger savings offset by some losses; this decomposition is unknown.\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.3cm}p{1.9cm}p{4.0cm}@{}}\n\\toprule\nStatement & Status & Evidence and scope\\\\\n\\midrule\nH1: solvers beat naive baselines & Supported & Held-out main efficiency 1.0032 vs.\\ 0.8166; two boards, two seeds; planner pool includes the naive plan\\\\\nH2: polish lowers cost & Small gain, unreplicated & Development combined 1.00339 vs.\\ 1.00183; two seeds; about 4$\\times$ evaluator wall-clock\\\\\nH3: MILP layer lowers cost & Not supported & Development metrics identical to base; cause not recoverable\\\\\nRequired investment varies by rule & Not measured & No per-rule costs retained\\\\","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"06f24fa2-63d3-4bc9-bb45-b72aa78f77ec","uuid":"a0dc104d-f7c3-435d-8651-168718d94e65","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:32:04.675Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"201: title = {GPQA: A Graduate-Level Google-Proof Q\\&A Benchmark},\n\\emph{Verification before return.} Discrete rules flip at exact floating-point ties, and heuristics that shave a plan to the boundary can land on the wrong side by rounding. Returning only plans that an exact replica of the rule confirms removes this failure mode at the cost of extra evaluations, and it makes the planner's output trustworthy independently of the solver that proposed it. \\emph{Rule-specific structure.} A uniform gain ignores which tasks are cheap to lift; solvers that exploit the structure of each rule (Table~\\ref{tab:rules}) should find cheaper plans than a rule-agnostic fill. \\emph{Boundary polishing.} A plan returned by a constructive heuristic is rarely tight, so shrinking each gain to the verification boundary, dropping redundant tasks and moving points between tasks should lower cost further, and for L0 two-for-one swaps can reduce the number of treated tasks. We state three hypotheses:\n\\begin{description}\n\\item[H1 (solver advantage).] Verified per-rule solvers have higher main efficiency than the better of uniform-gain and greedy marginal-cost baselines on held-out boards.\n\\item[H2 (polish).] Boundary polishing lowers verified plan cost relative to the base solvers on a development leaderboard.\n\\item[H3 (MILP selection).] A MILP level-selection layer lowers cost for the discrete rules (Borda, Copeland, plurality) and the trimmed mean relative to the base solvers.\n\\end{description}\nH1 is the only hypothesis tested on held-out data. H2 and H3 are tested only in the development comparison with two seeds. The question of rule-dependence in required investment is not among the tested hypotheses (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n\\caption{Held-out evaluation on two Open LLM Leaderboard boards (two evaluator seeds). (a) Main efficiency of the verified planner and of the better of two naive baselines per case, against the reference level of 1. (b) Main, control and combined efficiency of the planner on a zoomed axis; dots are the two per-seed combined values.}\n\\label{fig:final}\n\\end{figure}\n\nThe held-out evaluation completed and its outcome is qualified. The planner's main efficiency is 1.0032 relative to the reference solver, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.main_efficiency]]\nversus 0.8166 for the better naive baseline per case, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_main_efficiency]]\na difference of 0.187 (Figure~\\ref{fig:final}a). The plan validity rate is 1.0, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.plan_validity_rate]]\nso no episode was malformed, timed out or out of bounds. Control efficiency is 1.0002, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_efficiency]]\nand the combined score is 1.0026. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.combined_score]]\nPer-seed combined scores were 1.00260 and 1.00257 for seeds 9001 and 9002, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\nand each seed individually satisfies the gate that main efficiency exceeds the baseline's.\n\nThree observations bound the interpretation. First, an efficiency near 1 means that the planner's verified plans cost, on average, about the same as the reference plans; the retained value does not reveal how many cases sit exactly at 1, how many are above, or whether any case is below. Second, the planner's candidate set includes the uniform-gain and headroom-fill plans, so a large share of the gap to the baselines is expected from per-rule solvers; the comparison shows that the solvers matter for cost, not that this planner is better than other sophisticated planners, which were not evaluated. Third, the two evaluator seeds do not resample the leaderboard: they change permutation and shuffle draws and restart randomness, so the 3.5$\\times10^{-5}$ spread between seeds reflects solver and control variability, not data sampling, and no confidence interval or significance test can be formed from two values.\n\n\\paragraph{Reading an efficiency near 1.} The held-out main efficiency averages 64 main cases per seed (8 rules, 2 target ranks, 2 cost measures, 2 boards), each with weight $1/64\\approx0.0156$. An excess of 0.0032 over 1 is therefore about one fifth of the credit a single case would add at the maximal per-episode efficiency of 2, and about 0.2 of a case-unit in total. The retained aggregate cannot distinguish many tiny savings relative to the reference from a few larger savings offset by some losses; this decomposition is unknown.\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.3cm}p{1.9cm}p{4.0cm}@{}}\n\\toprule\nStatement & Status & Evidence and scope\\\\\n\\midrule\nH1: solvers beat naive baselines & Supported & Held-out main efficiency 1.0032 vs.\\ 0.8166; two boards, two seeds; planner pool includes the naive plan\\\\\nH2: polish lowers cost & Small gain, unreplicated & Development combined 1.00339 vs.\\ 1.00183; two seeds; about 4$\\times$ evaluator wall-clock\\\\\nH3: MILP layer lowers cost & Not supported & Development metrics identical to base; cause not recoverable\\\\\nRequired investment varies by rule & Not measured & No per-rule costs retained\\\\","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvufuxKitJZ5HzMZnYAX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014zJPfuFuFVyookooo48Wku","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'E'\np='paper.tex'; s=open(p).read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert a in s, a[:50]; s=s.replace(a,b)\nrep(r\"\\item[H1 (solver advantage).] Verified per-rule solvers have higher main efficiency than the better of uniform-gain and greedy marginal-cost baselines on held-out boards.\",\nr\"\\item[H1 (planner advantage).] The frozen verified planner (base solvers, verification, pruning and boundary polishing) has higher main efficiency than the better of the evaluator's uniform-gain and greedy marginal-cost baselines on held-out boards. The held-out study evaluates the planner as a whole, so H1 does not isolate the per-rule solvers from verification, pruning, polishing or the uniform-gain fallback.\")\nrep(r\"so a large share of the gap to the baselines is expected from per-rule solvers; the comparison shows that the solvers matter for cost, not that this planner is better than other sophisticated planners, which were not evaluated.\",\nr\"and the comparison is between the whole frozen planner (base solvers, verification, pruning, boundary polishing and the uniform-gain fallback) and the evaluator's naive baselines. No held-out ablation separates the per-rule solvers from those other components, so the gap of 0.187 cannot be attributed to the solvers specifically (in development, polishing alone moved main efficiency from 1.0031 to 1.0051). The comparison also does not show that this planner is better than other sophisticated planners, which were not evaluated.\")\nrep(r\"H1: solvers beat naive baselines & Supported & Held-out main efficiency 1.0032 vs.\\ 0.8166; two boards, two seeds; planner pool includes the naive plan\\\\\",\nr\"H1: frozen planner beats naive baselines & Supported for the whole planner & Held-out main efficiency 1.0032 vs.\\ 0.8166; two boards, two seeds; no held-out ablation isolates the per-rule solvers; pool includes the naive plan\\\\\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nE\ngrep -n -i \"solver advantage\\|solvers matter\\|solvers beat\" paper.tex claims_grounding.json; grep -n \"1.0051\\|search_metrics\" claims_grounding.json | head"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2140,"cache_read_input_tokens":53704,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2140},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"06f24fa2-63d3-4bc9-bb45-b72aa78f77ec","uuid":"319b0267-95fc-4df6-8920-0e4a275c8195","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:32:11.854Z","request_id":"req_011CgvufuMNTLwaLMuxyS7wq"}
