--- final message ---
I fixed both hard errors from the last validation round. `paper.tex` now compiles cleanly, and the manuscript no longer credits the held-out gain to the per-rule solvers. I didn't run the full validation suite or the agentic evidence audit again; the checks I did run are listed below.

- **Audit finding (solver attribution):** H1 and its row in the hypothesis-status table now say that the whole frozen planner beats the better naive baseline on the two held-out boards. The planner is the base solvers plus verification, pruning, boundary polishing and the uniform-gain fallback. I removed the sentence "the comparison shows that the solvers matter for cost". In its place the text says that no held-out ablation separates the per-rule solvers from those other components. It also notes that in development, polishing alone moved main efficiency from 1.0031 to 1.0051. I updated the matching entry in `claims_grounding.json` to the same wording.
- **LaTeX failure:** The GPQA title in `references.bib` had an unescaped `&` ("Q&A"), which broke the bibliography build. I escaped it and reran the full pdflatex, bibtex, pdflatex, pdflatex cycle. The paper builds with no errors and no undefined citations or references. My `pdflatex` runs report 13 pages in total, with the Conclusion on page 9 and the Supplementary Information starting on page 12. I couldn't check the body-length rule because `pdftotext` isn't installed.
- **Evidence trail:** The scientific-evidence verifier reports 0 dangling references (46 total, 21 resolved, 25 anchored).
- **Memory:** I logged the repair in `memory.md`, `global_memory/memory.md` and `global_memory/memory.jsonl`.

